---
/**
 * WhyCaail — the same question, answered from memory and from an index.
 *
 * Layout: centred header, then one seamless two-column panel whose halves each carry a
 * mock exchange at the top and a bulleted list beneath, with a bold claim and a muted
 * sub-line per bullet.
 *
 * ONE panel split down the middle, not two panels with a gap. The section IS a
 * comparison, so the halves belong inside a single frame and the divider carries the
 * contrast. It also removes a class of alignment bug: grid children of one container
 * stretch to a shared height, whereas two separately bordered boxes are only ever the
 * same height by coincidence.
 *
 * REGISTER (CAAIL-30). The columns name the two INFORMATION SOURCES, not the presence or
 * absence of a product. That is not a softening: it is what actually differs. The same
 * assistant answers both times, and the only variable is whether the answer was
 * reconstructed from training data or read out of a table. "Without CAAIL" / "With CAAIL"
 * asserted a benefit before the reader had seen a single line of evidence, which for an
 * audience of working scientists costs more credibility than it buys attention. Every
 * bullet here is a mechanism, checkable against the corpus or against how decoding works.
 *
 * The columns are NOT rows, and their lengths differ on purpose. They are two independent
 * lists of properties, and the uneven counts are the signal that no row correspondence is
 * implied.
 *
 * EVERY RENDERED FIGURE IS DERIVED at build time. No number the reader sees is typed here,
 * and none may be: this band is the least forgivable place in the repository for a figure
 * that no longer matches the corpus. The open-access percentage that used to sit in the
 * licence bullet was typed by hand and quoted a denominator (works with an OpenAlex record)
 * different from the one in the same sentence (all references), which is exactly the
 * failure it now cannot express.
 *
 * The lede says "in this section" rather than "on this page" ADVISEDLY. The guards below
 * cover this component and nothing else, and the claim is currently false page-wide:
 * `Hero.astro` renders `counts.papers` labelled "papers" when 345 is six sections, only 229
 * of them primary research. Widening the sentence would be asserting a property of code
 * this file cannot see.
 *
 * The tints are ~4% washes carrying no meaning on their own. The header text does the
 * semantic work, because a hue difference is not something every reader can see. The
 * bullet marks are deliberately IDENTICAL on both sides: once the columns stopped being
 * good-and-bad, a tick against one list and a cross against the other would have put the
 * verdict back that the copy had just removed.
 */
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import matrix from '../../public/api/matrix.json';
import papers from '../../public/api/papers.json';
import catalog from '../../public/api/catalog.json';
import { licenseTier } from '../lib/licenses';
import counts from '../content/data/counts.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

/* A POPULATED cell, deliberately.
   The empty-cell demonstration moved to the worked-queries band. Absence is CAAIL's
   subtlest claim and it makes a poor opener: "we return nothing, but honestly" is a
   harder sell than "we return citations you can click, with ids". The everyday contrast,
   invented references versus real ones, is the one every reader has already been
   burned by.

   And a cell NO OTHER SECTION USES. This has now moved twice for that reason, which is the
   thing to know before moving it again. It was Bayesian Optimization × Media Optimization
   until a worked-query card took that cell; it then became Ensemble Learning × Sensory
   Prediction, chosen on the reasoning that "none of the four worked queries touches
   sensory" — and the very next rewrite of that band made its fourth card a sensory card
   naming this exact cell, while `#why` asked "which papers used ensemble models to predict
   flavour or texture" two hundred words above a card asking which methods predict sensory
   outcomes from instrumental measurements. Two bands, one cell, nearly one question.

   The claim "no other section uses it" is therefore not a fact about this file, and no
   comment here can keep it true. `homepage-agent.spec.ts` asserts it instead, by reading
   the cell this band advertises and failing if the `#ask` band's rendered text names it.
   Move this cell and that test tells you immediately whether you have collided again.

   Deep Learning × Cellular Engineering is the largest cell in an application column that no
   card argues from, and enough of it links code for the "runnable code" line to say
   something. Card 2 owns the media column and card 4 the sensory one; cards 1 and 3 read
   the dataset inventory and the catalogue and touch no cell at all. */
const METHOD = 'Deep Learning';
const AREA = 'cell';
const cell = matrix.cells.find((c) => c.method === METHOD && c.area === AREA);
if (!cell) throw new Error(`WhyCaail: ${METHOD} × ${AREA} is not in matrix.json`);
const AREA_LABEL = cell.areaLabel;
if (cell.refIds.length < 3) {
  throw new Error(
    `WhyCaail: ${METHOD} × ${AREA} has only ${cell.refIds.length} paper(s). The homepage ` +
      `example quotes real citations from this cell — pick a better populated cell or rewrite it.`,
  );
}

const byId = new Map(papers.references.map((r) => [r.id, r]));
const cited = cell.refIds.slice(0, 3).map((id) => {
  const r = byId.get(id);
  const who = (r?.authors?.[0] ?? '').split(',')[0] || 'Unknown';
  return `${who} ${r?.year ?? ''} (#${id})`;
});
const withCode = cell.refIds.filter((id) => byId.get(id)?.hasCode).length;

/* The closing line promises every reference id above resolves to an entry with its DOI.
   That is a property of THIS CELL, not of the corpus: three references carry no DOI. The
   sentence holds today and would go silently false the moment METHOD/AREA is repointed to
   a cell containing one of them, which is the one edit this component invites. */
const citedWithoutDoi = cell.refIds.filter((id) => !byId.get(id)?.doi);
if (citedWithoutDoi.length > 0) {
  throw new Error(
    `WhyCaail: reference(s) ${citedWithoutDoi.join(', ')} in ${METHOD} × ${AREA} carry no DOI, ` +
      `so the closing line "every reference id above resolves to an entry with its DOI" is ` +
      `false. Pick a different cell, or rewrite that line.`,
  );
}

/* The reference sections, counted separately.
   `papers.references` is SIX sections, not one: primary research, reviews and
   perspectives, and four reference works. Quoting its length as "papers" is the
   population-mislabelling failure CAAIL-12 was filed about, and it overstates the
   primary corpus by half. The lede names each population instead of summing them. */
const primary = papers.references.filter((r) => r.section === 'References');
const reviews = papers.references.filter((r) => r.section === 'Reviews & Perspectives');
const refWorks = papers.references.filter((r) => r.section.endsWith('Reference Work'));

/* The three counted populations must exhaust the corpus. A seventh section added to
   Papers.md would otherwise vanish from the lede silently, and the lede would quietly
   become a smaller number than the file it describes. */
if (primary.length + reviews.length + refWorks.length !== papers.references.length) {
  throw new Error(
    `WhyCaail: the lede counts ${primary.length + reviews.length + refWorks.length} of ` +
      `${papers.references.length} references. A reference section exists that the lede ` +
      `does not name; add it there rather than letting the page undercount the corpus.`,
  );
}

/* The lede says every primary-research paper is placed in at least one matrix cell.
   `isPrimary` is exactly that conjunction, so if the two counts ever diverge the sentence
   has become false and this build should fail instead of publishing it. */
const placed = papers.references.filter((r) => r.isPrimary).length;
if (placed !== primary.length) {
  throw new Error(
    `WhyCaail: ${primary.length} references sit under "References" but ${placed} are cited ` +
      `by a matrix cell. The lede claims every primary paper is placed; fix the matrix or ` +
      `the lede, not this check.`,
  );
}

/* The licence figures are about SOFTWARE, not papers.
   They used to report how many papers were free to read versus free to redistribute. That
   is the academic form of the licence question, and it answers the wrong one for much of
   this audience: cell ag is full of private companies, and what they need to know is
   whether they may build on a tool and what a derivative inherits if they do. A paper's
   open-access status says nothing about that. (The paper axis is not lost; `isOa` is still
   derived onto every reference and served in the agent API.)

   COPYLEFT IS THE POINT. It is the one figure here that changes what a commercial team is
   allowed to ship, and no paper-level number expresses it.

   Software only, deliberately. Databases record a licence for 36 of 150 and dataset
   entries for none at all, so folding them in would report the state of our curation
   rather than the state of the field. Their backfill is tracked separately; until it
   lands, a number spanning them would mislead in the direction that flatters us. */
const tools = catalog.software;
const byTier = (t: string) => tools.filter((s) => licenseTier(s.license) === t).length;
const permissive = byTier('permissive');
const copyleft = byTier('copyleft');
const restricted = byTier('restricted');
const unrecorded = byTier('unknown');
if (permissive + copyleft + restricted + unrecorded !== tools.length) {
  throw new Error(
    `WhyCaail: the four licence tiers account for ${permissive + copyleft + restricted + unrecorded} ` +
      `of ${tools.length} tools. A tier exists that this bullet does not name.`,
  );
}
if (copyleft === 0) {
  throw new Error(
    'WhyCaail: no catalogued tool is copyleft, so the bullet\'s "a derivative inherits the ' +
      'licence" clause describes nothing. Rewrite it rather than shipping an empty warning.',
  );
}

/* The bullet names the types instead of counting them, and reads the names from here.
   It said "all four content types", which was a typed numeral beside a rendered
   `${recorded.length}` and would have drifted the day a fifth type is catalogued.
   "Every content type" would be the easy fix and is an OVERCLAIM: talks, primers,
   reference works and funding are content and carry no topic tag. These four are exactly
   the catalogue the subject axis spans, so listing them is both derived and precise. */
const CONTENT_TYPES = ['papers', 'software', 'databases', 'dataset entries'];
const contentTypeList = `${CONTENT_TYPES.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and ${CONTENT_TYPES.at(-1)}`;

/* Short on purpose. This is a chat bubble, not a worked query: the #ask band is where the
   three-sentence research questions live. What this one has to be is fabricable, meaning
   a question whose plausible wrong answer is indistinguishable from a right one. */
const Q = 'Which papers applied deep learning to cell-line engineering for cultivated meat, and which of them released code I can run?';

/* Properties of an answer reconstructed from training data. Each states a mechanism of
   how decoding works, not a complaint about model quality: a better model has every one
   of these properties too, which is the point. */
const fromMemory = [
  {
    k: 'A citation is generated, not retrieved',
    v: 'The reference is produced token by token, so a well-formed DOI is not evidence that the work exists',
  },
  {
    k: 'Ask twice, get two answers',
    v: 'Decoding samples from a distribution, so a pinned model on the same day need not agree with itself',
  },
  {
    k: 'There is no result for "found none"',
    v: 'A gap in the training data and a gap in the field produce the same reply',
  },
  {
    k: 'Frequency stands in for relevance',
    v: 'What is heavily cited and plainly titled is what is recalled; a tool with an obscure name is not',
  },
  {
    k: 'Reuse terms do not travel with the answer',
    v: 'A tool arrives named but unlicensed, so nothing separates one you can ship from one whose terms your product would inherit',
  },
];

/* Properties of an answer read out of the index. Every figure below is derived above. */
const fromIndex = [
  {
    k: `All ${matrix.totalCells} method × area cells are enumerated`,
    v: `${matrix.emptyCells} hold no indexed paper, and each is returned as an empty result rather than skipped`,
  },
  {
    k: 'An empty cell is returned with its own limit attached',
    v: 'A curated subset, not a census: CAAIL has not measured its own recall',
  },
  {
    k: 'Entries are placed by a curator, not ranked by a signal',
    v: 'An obscure tool and a well-known one are listed on the same basis, because inclusion is a recorded judgement',
  },
  {
    k: `One subject axis across ${contentTypeList}`,
    v: 'A design question returns them together, not one type at a time',
  },
  {
    /* The headline must not promise a tier on every tool: some record none, and that
       number is rendered in the same breath. Naming the gap in the claim also matches how
       the rest of this column treats absence. */
    k: 'A licence tier per tool, and a stated gap where there is none',
    v: `Of ${tools.length} software tools, ${permissive} are permissive and ${copyleft} copyleft, so a derivative inherits the terms; ${restricted} are restricted and ${unrecorded} record none`,
  },
  {
    k: 'The same query returns the same rows',
    v: `A fixed corpus dated ${matrix.corpusDate}, so a result can be quoted and re-checked by someone else`,
  },
];
---

<section class="why" id="why">
  <div class="hd sr">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="bar" aria-hidden="true"></span>Why CAAIL</p>
    <h2 class="caail-display">The same question, answered from memory and from an index</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      CAAIL is a static index an AI agent can query directly. It holds {primary.length} primary-research
      papers, each placed in at least one cell of a {matrix.methods.length} × {matrix.areas.length} matrix
      of AI method against research area, alongside {reviews.length} reviews and perspectives,
      {refWorks.length} reference-work entries, {counts.software} software tools, {counts.databases} databases
      and {counts.datasets} dataset entries. Every figure in this section is read from that corpus when
      the page is built.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="split sr sr-d1">
    <div class="half half--memory">
      <p class="hdr">
        <span class="badge badge--memory"><Icon name="lucide:brain" /></span> Answered from model memory
      </p>

      <div class="chat">
        <div class="turn"><span class="who">You</span><p class="bubble">{Q}</p></div>
        <div class="turn">
          <span class="who who--ai">AI</span>
          <p class="bubble bubble--guess">
            Convolutional and transformer models have both been used to guide cell-line
            engineering, for example Zhang et al. (2021) in
            <em>Metab. Eng.</em>… <em>[nothing here tells you whether this exists]</em>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul class="pts">
        {fromMemory.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <span class="mark mark--memory" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span><strong>{p.k}</strong><span class="sub">{p.v}</span></span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="half half--index">
      <p class="hdr">
        <span class="badge badge--index"><Icon name="lucide:table-2" /></span> Answered from CAAIL
      </p>

      <div class="chat">
        <div class="turn"><span class="who who--dark">You</span><p class="bubble bubble--dark">{Q}</p></div>
        <div class="turn">
          <span class="who who--tool">API</span>
          {/* DERIVED, never spelled out. This read "× Media Optimization" as a literal
              while METHOD and AREA were constants above, so changing the example cell
              left the mock advertising one cell and answering another. */}
          <p class="bubble bubble--tool"><code>matrix.json → {METHOD} × {AREA_LABEL}</code></p>
        </div>
        <div class="turn">
          <span class="who who--ai">AI</span>
          <p class="bubble">
            {/* "and N more" is suppressed at N === 0. The guard above admits a cell with
                exactly 3 refs, which rendered "3 papers: A, B, C and 0 more." Not reachable
                with the current cell, but repointing METHOD/AREA is the one edit this
                component invites, so it should not be able to produce that. */}
            {cell.refIds.length} papers: {cited.join(', ')}{cell.refIds.length > 3
              ? ` and ${cell.refIds.length - 3} more`
              : ''}.
            {withCode} link runnable code. <em class="ok">[ids resolve, corpus dated {matrix.corpusDate}]</em>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul class="pts">
        {fromIndex.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <span class="mark mark--index" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span><strong>{p.k}</strong><span class="sub">{p.v}</span></span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* The disclosure that used to live only in a code comment. A constructed example is a
      legitimate device; an unlabelled one asks the reader to take our word for both halves,
      which is the habit this section exists to argue against. */}
  <p class="note sr sr-d2">
    The left exchange is constructed and its citation is invented: it illustrates the failure,
    it does not report one. The right exchange is generated when this page is built, from the
    same <code>matrix.json</code> an agent fetches.
  </p>

  <p class="foot sr sr-d2">
    Every reference id above resolves to an entry with its DOI.
    <a href={`${base}/papers/explorer/`}>Browse the matrix →</a>
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .why { margin: var(--caail-section-gap) 0 0; }

  .hd { text-align: center; max-width: 44rem; margin: 0 auto var(--caail-space-6); }
  .eyebrow {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .55rem;
    font-size: var(--caail-fs-xs); letter-spacing: .18em; text-transform: uppercase;
    font-weight: 600; color: var(--caail-muted); margin: 0 0 var(--caail-space-3);
  }
  .bar { display: block; width: 2px; height: .85rem; background: var(--caail-link); border-radius: 2px; }
  .why h2 { font-size: var(--caail-fs-h2); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -.02em; color: var(--caail-primary); margin: 0 0 var(--caail-space-3); text-wrap: balance; }
  .lede { font-size: var(--caail-fs-lg); color: var(--caail-muted); margin: 0; text-wrap: pretty; }

  /* One frame, no gap. overflow:hidden lets the tints run into the rounded corners
     instead of squaring them off. */
  .split {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--caail-border); border-radius: 16px; overflow: hidden;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgb(0 0 0 / .07), 0 2px 4px -2px rgb(0 0 0 / .07);
  }
  @media (min-width: 52rem) { .split { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }

  .half { padding: var(--caail-space-6); }
  /* One internal edge that pivots with the layout: a horizontal rule when stacked, a
     vertical one when side by side. */
  .half--memory { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--caail-border); background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--caail-area-sensory) 4%, transparent); }
  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .half--memory { border-bottom: 0; border-right: 1px solid var(--caail-border); }
  }
  .half--index { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--caail-link) 5%, transparent); }

  .hdr {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .55rem;
    font-size: var(--caail-fs-xs); letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase;
    font-weight: 700; color: var(--caail-muted); margin: 0 0 var(--caail-space-5);
  }
  .badge { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; width: 1.6rem; height: 1.6rem; border-radius: 50%; flex: none; }
  .badge [data-icon] { width: .9rem; height: .9rem; }
  .badge--memory { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--caail-ink) 10%, transparent); color: var(--caail-muted); }
  .badge--index { background: var(--caail-primary); color: var(--caail-bg); }
  .half--index .hdr { color: var(--caail-primary); }

  /* Mock exchange. A transcript shape, not a real one: it illustrates the difference
     between the two answers rather than claiming to be a captured session. */
  .chat { background: var(--caail-bg); border: 1px solid var(--caail-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: var(--caail-space-4); margin: 0 0 var(--caail-space-5); display: grid; gap: .55rem; }
  .turn { display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: .5rem; }
  .who {
    flex: none; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    width: 1.5rem; height: 1.5rem; border-radius: 50%;
    font-size: .58rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .02em;
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--caail-ink) 12%, transparent); color: var(--caail-muted);
  }
  .who--dark { background: var(--caail-primary); color: var(--caail-bg); }
  .who--ai { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--caail-ink) 18%, transparent); color: var(--caail-ink); }
  .who--tool { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--caail-area-bioprocess) 22%, transparent); color: var(--caail-accent); }

  .bubble { margin: 0; font-size: var(--caail-fs-sm); line-height: 1.5; color: var(--caail-ink); background: var(--caail-surface); border-radius: 10px; padding: .45rem .7rem; }
  .bubble--dark { background: var(--caail-primary); color: var(--caail-bg); }
  .bubble--guess { font-style: italic; color: var(--caail-muted); }
  .bubble--tool { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--caail-area-bioprocess) 10%, transparent); }
  .bubble--tool code { font-size: var(--caail-fs-xs); color: var(--caail-accent); background: none; padding: 0; }
  /* Emphasis by weight and word, not hue alone: the caveat reads as a caveat in
     monochrome too. */
  .bubble em { font-style: normal; font-weight: 600; color: var(--caail-ink); }
  .bubble em.ok { color: var(--caail-primary); }

  /* The claim line's type metrics, declared ONCE. Both the text and the marker that has to
     centre on its first line read them from here, so "the dot follows the type scale" is a
     property of the code rather than of two hand-copied values agreeing. */
  .pts {
    list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--caail-space-4);
    --pt-fs: var(--caail-fs-sm); --pt-lh: 1.4;
  }
  .pts li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 1fr; gap: .6rem; align-items: start; margin: 0; }
  /* Same shape both sides. A dot marks where a list item starts and says nothing else,
     which is the whole intent: the column header carries the meaning, so the marker is
     not asked to pass a verdict the copy no longer makes.

     Positioned by a LINE BOX, never by a margin. `starlight-overrides.css` zeroes
     `margin-top` on every direct child of `.why .pts li` (the prose-flow trap documented
     there), so a margin here is silently dropped and the dot floats above the text it
     marks. Instead the span is exactly one line of `strong` tall, in `strong`'s own font
     metrics, and centres the dot inside, which lands on the first line's centre with no
     margin involved and stays there if the type scale moves. */
  .mark {
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    width: .4rem; flex: none;
    font-size: var(--pt-fs); line-height: var(--pt-lh); height: calc(1em * var(--pt-lh));
  }
  .mark::before { content: ''; width: .4rem; height: .4rem; border-radius: 50%; background: currentColor; }
  .mark--memory { color: var(--caail-area-sensory); }
  .mark--index { color: var(--caail-primary); }

  .pts strong { display: block; font-size: var(--pt-fs); font-weight: 600; color: var(--caail-ink); line-height: var(--pt-lh); }
  .pts .sub { display: block; font-size: var(--caail-fs-xs); color: var(--caail-muted); line-height: 1.45; margin-top: .15rem; }

  .note, .foot { font-size: var(--caail-fs-sm); color: var(--caail-muted); margin: var(--caail-space-5) auto 0; max-width: var(--caail-measure); line-height: 1.55; text-align: center; text-wrap: pretty; }
  .note { font-size: var(--caail-fs-xs); }
  .note code { font-size: inherit; background: none; padding: 0; color: var(--caail-accent); }
  .foot { margin-top: var(--caail-space-3); }
  .foot a { white-space: nowrap; }
</style>
